  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nIsolated showers will continue across NE TN and SW VA this \nafternoon with little in the way of sensible impacts from them. Our \ncold front has moved through the area and will usher in colder \ntemperatures over the next half day or so. The upper level low will \npivot over the Ohio Valley and S Great Lakes region over the next \nday or so before moving off the East Coast. For tonight Low \ntemperatures will fall in the mid 30s for the Valley and upper 20s \nto lower 30s in the mountains. The colder air will allow rain \nshowers to changeover to snow showers across in the higher \nelevations and E TN mountains. Very light accumulations are possible \ntonight, but the best chance of accumulating snow arrives Friday \nafternoon. \n\nOn Friday, our attention turns to the northwest flow set up over the \nregion. Streamlines support our 850mb flow coming through the \nwestern Great Lakes into the TN Valley. This is a favorable flow \npattern for upslope flow precipitation events in our area. Our 850mb \nwind speeds will be between 20 to 25 knots, sufficient for \norographic lift and snow showers. Forecast soundings show favorable \nlow level moisture that extends up nearly into the DGZ. Our low \nlevel temperatures aren't perfect, but should be cold enough for \nsnow with the added help of orographic lift. Our heavier, more \npersistent snow showers will begin Friday afternoon in the eastern \nTN mountains. \n\nWith that in mind, we've opted to nudge back the start time of the \nWinter Weather Advisory in the eastern TN mountains above 3000 feet \nto 15z Friday morning. This adjustment should better highlight the \nperiod of greatest potential winter impacts in the higher \nelevations. The final vort max will move through the region Friday \nevening. This disturbance will help amplify precipitation coverage \nwith more widespread rain showers in the Valleys and persistent snow \nshowers in the mountains. visibilities will likely plummet below \n1/2sm in the heaviest snow showers Friday evening. Snow totals have \ntrended ever so slightly higher. Most locations in the Winter \nWeather Advisory end up between 2 to 4 inch range. For the highest \nmountain peaks, locally higher amounts in the 4 to 6 inch range \nappear possible.\n\nOur upper level low will begin to exit the region on Saturday. This \nwill weaken our 850mb flow and cause precipitation to wrap up \nSaturday morning. Saturday and Sunday will be colder with highs in \nthe 40s and lower 50s. However, skies will begin to clear out \nSaturday afternoon and the wind speeds will decrease. Saturday night \nwill likely be the coldest night of the season so far with lows in \nthe upper 20s to lower 30s in the Valley and Plateau counties. High \npressure will build into the region and will lead to a gradual \nwarming trend Sunday into next week. Highs will warm into the mid to \nupper 50s Monday and Tuesday with 60s expected mid week. The \nremainder of the forecast period after Saturday will be dry under \nthe influence of the ridge and high pressure.",
